## 5. What the data supports and what it does not

| Supported | Not supported without more data |
|---|---|
| The ARPDAU drop is explained by tier mix; within-tier change is indistinguishable from zero | Why the mix changed beyond the observed paid_video ramp |
| Observed 30- and 60-day IAP value per install by tier | Whether any tier is profitable to acquire (needs CPI) |
| No detectable channel difference in value at matched tier mix | Channel differences below ~$0.36–0.45 per install (~20%): too small for this sample to detect reliably |
| Lapse risk can be ranked on unseen weeks | How many lapses an offer prevents (needs a randomised holdout) |
| Players rarely reach the ad cap | The effect of changing the cap (cap assignment method unknown) |
| Revenue dips around each sale | Whether sales add or only move revenue (cadence never varied; needs an experiment) |

### Specific limits

- **Values are gross of app-store fees.** IAP value per install is what players paid, before the store's commission (typically 15–30%). Compare CPI with the net figure, e.g. $1.63 gross ≈ $1.14–1.39 net for Tier 4 at 60 days.
- **Ad revenue is missing.** Tier 4 players complete ~1.8× as many rewarded ads per active day as Tier 1, so IAP-only value understates them most.
- **No value beyond day 60.** The extract is too short to estimate lifetime value, and nothing is extrapolated.
- **The 60-day value comes from Jan–Feb installs.** These are mostly pre-ramp. Using it for today's paid_video Tier 4 installs relies on Part 3's finding that channels do not differ at the same tier.
- **The lapse value ($0.62) is an upper bound.** It is the 30-day IAP gap between players who stayed and those who lapsed in the highest-risk fifth. Stayers are more engaged to begin with, so it is not a causal effect.
- **Cap assignment is unknown.** The cap-5 vs cap-8 comparison assumes caps are not tied to the type of player or day.
